\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{What the structure fixes and what it leaves free}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Which features follow from a chosen finite structure, and which
remain additional inputs?}

\p{Part VII compared the discrete physics with a continuous space
that was supplied. The [supplied-circle limit](fdip-004J) controls
one fixed Fourier mode. Part XIII has since built cochains and
their Hodge theory, a distance read from a commutator, and exact
coarse-graining of a hierarchical precision. Whether the physics
of continuous space comes back at large scales without being
supplied can now be asked. Its answer is left to a separate
plan. This section does not answer it, and it draws no continuum
limit.}

\p{Wilson's finite groups stay first. The tested claims are the two
finite maps of \ref{fdip-00A0}: conjugation #{SU(2)\to SO(3)} and
the quotient #{2T\to A_4}, both with kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. Those maps
fix the named kernels. The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain
open tests. The absence of a construction is not a no-go theorem
and not a physical identification.}

\p{Several programmes outside this forest have not been read: a
spectral derivation of the Standard Model, causal sets, loop
quantum gravity, and the string landscape. This note does not
record what they fix or leave free.}

\p{The [Euler product](fdip-008P), for
#{\operatorname{Re}s>1}, is the product over primes not dividing
#{2\det Q} of #{\,(1-\hat Z_p(Q)\,p^{-s})^{-1}}. It equals
#{L(s,\chi_D)} times the product, over primes dividing
#{2\det Q} but not #{D}, of #{\,(1-\chi_D(p)\,p^{-s})}. The
[Gaussian induced action](fdip-005R) takes the weight to be half
the logarithm of a determinant. The [cycle scaling](fdip-005X)
sends #{(\log\det Q_n)/2-n\log n} to a limit on the cycle. These
are not the same object. This note does not identify the Euler
product with that logarithm or with that limit.}

\p{The operator-algebra notes replace a supplied conditional
expectation by the composition law for nested expectations
(\ref{fdip-008U}), and they replace a supplied fixed-point
algebra by the image of the average (\ref{fdip-008X}). The
crossing block sets the reflection rank (\ref{fdip-008W}). None
of these is a continuum reconstruction.}

\p{On every square torus the Betti numbers are #{(1,2,1)}
(\ref{fdip-008Z}), and the harmonic cochains realize them
(\ref{fdip-0090}). The ordered compositions of exterior
multiplication and contraction span the endomorphisms of the
exterior algebra (\ref{fdip-0092}). The cup product obeys the
graded Leibniz rule and is not graded-commutative on cochains
(\ref{fdip-0093}). The note does not claim that the resulting
Clifford representation is injective.}

\p{The Connes distance is computed on the stated finite triples
(\ref{fdip-0095}). The edge-source triple makes the distance the
graph distance when each vertex has at most one incoming edge, and
on one grid it lies strictly between the Euclidean distance and
the graph distance (\ref{fdip-0096}). On a triangle-free graph
the gauge-dependent part of the fourth power of the massless
covariant Laplacian is 8 times the sum of
#{\cos\varphi_c-1} over the undirected 4-cycles
(\ref{fdip-00A7}). The comparison of that trace with the
logarithm of a massive determinant remains speculative. Neither
finite trace is identified with a continuum limit.}

\p{Nested residue classes diagonalize a hierarchical precision
(\ref{fdip-0099}). Block means of that precision keep a
hierarchical second-moment matrix (\ref{fdip-009A}). For
geometric coefficients, relevance of the top coefficient holds
only for #{0<\lambda<1}. A hierarchical Gaussian has site fourth
moment #{3\sigma^4} (\ref{fdip-009B}). No non-Gaussian
hierarchical fixed point is proved. These matrix relations do not
by themselves produce a Gaussian measure on functions on the
#{p}-adic integers.}

\p{Surface gluing of the finite-group state sum is convolution of
class functions (\ref{fdip-00A8}). That convolution is not
identified with the spatial transfer operator. On a finite
incidence presentation, invariant tensors count the gauge orbits
(\ref{fdip-00A9}). One square in #{M_4(\mathbb C)} cuts out 6
contexts with no global point, while each context separately has
a character (\ref{fdip-009E}). Whether that obstruction
organises the selectors remains open (\ref{fdip-009D}).
Subgroup action groupoids form a groupoid-valued presheaf on the
opposite of the subgroup poset (\ref{fdip-009F}). No stack is
proved.}
